WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.823
Sensitive data protection requires access control, not only encryption.

2
00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:16.458
Encryption can protect data at rest or in transit, but it does not decide who may read a record, download an export, view a tenant, approve a workflow, or run an administrative report.

3
00:00:16.458 --> 00:00:19.061
Authentication identifies the caller.

4
00:00:19.061 --> 00:00:23.348
Authorization decides what data or action that caller may access.

5
00:00:23.348 --> 00:00:32.764
Resource-level checks may need ownership, tenant membership, role, claim, scope, subscription, record status, or business-rule evaluation.

6
00:00:32.764 --> 00:00:36.285
The right check depends on the resource and operation.

7
00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:43.864
Tenant IDs and user IDs supplied by clients must be checked against trusted server-side data and validated identity.

8
00:00:43.864 --> 00:00:49.911
A request parameter should not become the authority for which tenant or user data is returned.

9
00:00:49.911 --> 00:00:54.351
Server-side authorization should make the data boundary explicit.

10
00:00:54.351 --> 00:01:03.920
Least privilege should apply to application users, service identities, database accounts, support tools, background jobs, and administrators.

11
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:15.786
A background job that reads every tenant, a support tool with broad export rights, or a database account with unnecessary write access can expose more data than the main user interface.

12
00:01:15.786 --> 00:01:22.063
UI hiding, route naming, obscurity, or client-side checks are not server-side data protection.

13
00:01:22.063 --> 00:01:29.105
Support and administrative access should be logged, reviewed, and limited to legitimate operational need.

14
00:01:29.105 --> 00:01:33.010
Sensitive data access should be explainable after the fact.
